From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 09:26:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] sdc: write_sdc writes a standard collection an exception
 names exactly
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A constraint written with a standard collection, as asap7's platform
SDC writes its I/O and register budgets,

  set_max_delay -from [all_registers] -to [all_outputs] ...

is stored as the objects the collection named, and write_sdc wrote them
back one get_cells or get_ports at a time. On XiangShan's MemBlock that
is six exceptions over 268,694 registers and 5,000 ports: a 154 MB .sdc
of 1.65 million lines, read again by every later step.

Where an exception's -from or -to is exactly [all_registers], [all_outputs],
[all_inputs -no_clocks] or [all_inputs], the collection is written
instead; any other set is written as before. Read back on the same
netlist the collection names the same objects. all_registers without
options is every leaf instance with a register clock pin, which the
network answers without a timing graph, so writing builds none;
all_inputs -no_clocks is decided by the clocks, which write_sdc writes
before the exceptions.

MemBlock's synthesis .sdc goes from 154 MB to 1.5 KB. Read with the
read_sdc fix beside it, read_sdc took 24.0 s instead of 37.3 s and the
timing update after it 42.3 s instead of 65.9 s; worst and total
negative slack are the same, and writing the constraints back gives
byte-identical output either way.

sdc_write_collections checks all four collections and that a subset of
the registers is still written as a list. The exception tests whose
written SDC named both outputs of their design now read
-to [all_outputs].

Not upstreamed. Carried in bazel-orfs; retires on the //:bump onto an
OpenROAD whose OpenSTA has it.

Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
